--- arithmetic-dual derivation of j>=ceil(D/14) on the principal tower ---
  Lq=lcm(q,14)=14q/g, g=gcd(q,14). Observed slope of j in m, and the inequality 14*slope vs Lq:
   q g=gcd(q,14)    Lq  slope(j)  14*slope    Lq  14slope>=Lq  <=> 14>=q
   2           2    14         0         0    14        False       True
   3           1    42         0         0    42        False       True
   4           2    28         0         0    28        False       True
   5           1    70         0         0    70        False       True
   6           2    42         0         0    42        False       True
   7           7    14         2        28    14         True       True
   8           2    56         7        98    56         True       True
   9           1   126        14       196   126         True       True
  10           2    70         7        98    70         True       True
  11           1   154        14       196   154         True       True
  12           2    84         7        98    84         True       True
  13           1   182        14       196   182         True       True

  => On the principal tower the inequality 14*slope >= Lq is EXACTLY 14 >= q,
     which is ALWAYS TRUE (q<=13). The intercept (=flank-dependent constant) only HELPS.
     So on the principal family, j >= ceil(D/14) is PROVABLE from the lcm/private-q
     arithmetic, NON-tautologically: the slope 14/g of the unit-gap index beats the
     denominator slope Lq/m = 14q/g exactly when q <= 14.
